<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Federal Republic of Germany</h1><p class="st-meta">1949 CE – present · capital Bonn; later Berlin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Post-war German democracy, founded in the western zones in 1949 and enlarged by reunification in 1990.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The three western occupation zones were combined, and a parliamentary council drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basic-law-for-the-federal-republic-of-germany">Basic Law</a>, which came into force on 23 May 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a> became the first chancellor, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> as provisional capital, while the Soviet zone became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under the social market economy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-erhard">Ludwig Erhard</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> experienced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a>. It joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1955 and was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> in 1957. Willy Brandt's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostpolitik">Ostpolitik</a> eased relations with the East. The federal system gives strong powers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-of-germany">Länder</a>, and a Constitutional Court guards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basic-law-for-the-federal-republic-of-germany">Basic Law</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> in 1989, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">GDR</a> joined the Federal Republic on 3 October 1990 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> became the capital, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> is today the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union's</a> most populous member and largest economy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/federal-republic-of-germany.jpg" alt="Federal Republic of Germany" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: SKopp, Madden, and other users · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1949 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bonn; later Berlin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Konrad Adenauer, Willy Brandt, Helmut Kohl</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Germany, West Germany (1949-1990), Bundesrepublik Deutschland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1988&amp;focus=federal-republic-of-germany" aria-label="Federal Republic of Germany on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4907 409 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Federal Republic of Germany lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5227 665L5269 749M5227 665L5222 593M5227 665L5197 606M5227 665L5300 622M5227 665L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5269 749h0M5222 593h0M5197 606h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5227 665h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5227 665h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1988&amp;focus=federal-republic-of-germany">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
